= Intelligent agent =

== Overview ==
An '''intelligent agent''' is a computational entity modeled as perceiving an environment and selecting actions to pursue objectives.

The agent perspective is a foundational abstraction in artificial intelligence and multi-agent systems.

== Agent and environment ==
An agent is situated in an environment.

It receives observations or percepts and produces actions that can change the environment or the agent's own state.

A simple abstraction is:

<pre>
percepts / observations
        ↓
      agent
        ↓
      actions
</pre>

== Rationality ==
Classical AI often analyzes a rational agent as one that selects actions expected to achieve its objectives given its percepts, knowledge, and available actions.

Rationality is relative to information and objectives.

It does not mean omniscience or guaranteed success.

== Autonomy ==
Agents can differ in autonomy.

Some follow fixed rules.

Others learn, plan, adapt, or operate with limited direct human control.

[[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]] develops autonomy as a separate property.

== Not necessarily human-like ==
An intelligent agent need not have:

* natural language;
* consciousness;
* human-like reasoning;
* an LLM;
* persistent memory;
* external tools.

A thermostat-like controller, game agent, robot, or software agent can fit an agent model depending on the formalization.

== Modern AI agents ==
[[AI agent]] is a newer applied term frequently used for AI-powered systems that carry out multi-step tasks using models, tools, planning, memory, or environmental feedback.

That modern usage should not replace the broader classical agent abstraction.

== Related concepts ==
[[Artificial intelligence]], [[Perception]], [[Reasoning]], [[Planning (artificial intelligence)]], [[State machine]], [[System boundary]], [[Multi-agent system]], and [[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]] provide the surrounding agent model.

[[Feedback]] describes the closed-loop influence of prior outcomes on later behavior, while [[Adaptation (systems)]] describes changes in policy, state, or organization over time; an intelligent agent can use either without those concepts defining agenthood.

== References ==
* Stuart Russell & Peter Norvig, ''Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach'', agent chapters.
* Michael Wooldridge, ''An Introduction to MultiAgent Systems'', 2nd ed. (2009).
* Wooldridge & Jennings, foundational intelligent-agent literature.

